# Findings adjudicated and rejected, with the reason. One `<path>: <title>` per line.

# PR #69 (issue #60). Half of this does not hold: `followUpComplete` already requires
# `nextStep == null`, so a stale PENDING job blocks the completion marker on its own, with or
# without the paused flag. The other half is real but predates this PR and is independent of
# appointments: the job branch of the estimate does not mirror the handler's gates (agent.enabled,
# isTestSilenced, step existence, the episode check, shouldBotHandle), so a stale job shows a
# phantom countdown even with no appointment anywhere. That is a separate defect in the estimate
# itself and belongs in its own change, not folded into the appointment flag. Filed as #72.
src/modules/conversations/service.ts: Validate pending jobs before marking the sequence paused

# PR #76 (issue #65). The premise is right — the guardrail never sees the picture — but neither
# remedy is. Submitting bytes to it means a multimodal guardrail: the guardrails agent is text-only
# by construction (toxicity / unsafe content / competitor mentions / prompt adherence, all over a
# string), so this is a feature of its own, with its own model, cost and latency on the hot path.
# And suppressing images whenever they cannot be screened would disable the tool precisely for the
# operator careful enough to turn guardrails on, which inverts the incentive. An image already has
# its own mandatory, default-closed control: `sendImage.allowedHosts` is an operator decision, empty
# by default, and empty refuses every call — no image leaves without an explicit choice about its
# ORIGIN. What was genuinely missing is that the operator could not know the split, so the hint on
# that field now says guardrails read text and never the picture.
src/graph/runtime.ts: Screen image contents before delivering guarded output
